# Security Rules

Defines security expectations for storage, networking, logging, configuration, and UI handling.

## Mandatory rules

- Must not commit or log secrets, API keys, certificates, passwords, or tokens.
- Must use HTTPS for production communication unless a documented private-network exception exists.
- Must store sensitive session artifacts in secure storage; use `shared_preferences` only for non-sensitive settings.
- Must validate data at boundaries: UI input, repository input, API response, and local persistence.
- Must treat backend and local storage data as untrusted until validated or mapped, and must not display raw server errors to users.
- Must clear sensitive session state on logout.
- Must keep debug tools and verbose logs disabled in production builds.

## RBAC / ABAC authorization

- Must enforce RBAC and ABAC on every surface; unauthorized menus, components, actions, routes, and workflows must never be visible or accessible.
- Hidden UI alone is never sufficient; backend authorization is mandatory.
- Must treat [`permissions.mdc`](./permissions.mdc) and [`.cursor/access/`](../../.cursor/access/) as the source of truth.

## Secure storage notes

- Native mobile and desktop may use platform-backed secure storage.
- Web storage has limitations; must not assume it equals native secure storage, and must document any web auth/session strategy before production.

## Acceptance checklist

- Secret scanning finds no committed secrets.
- Production builds print no sensitive logs; failures handled safely.

